WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.980
<v Examiner>Waves of light and sound.

2
00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:03.671
<v Examiner>Four marks.

3
00:00:04.121 --> 00:00:08.792
<v Examiner>Explain amplitude, wavelength and frequency, and how we experience each one.

4
00:00:08.792 --> 00:00:10.636
<v Examiner>Compare light and sound waves,

5
00:00:10.636 --> 00:00:14.937
<v Examiner>and explain the difference between reflection and refraction of light.

6
00:00:15.387 --> 00:00:18.857
The hook: amplitude is how tall, so loud or bright.

7
00:00:18.857 --> 00:00:21.443
Wavelength is peak to peak, so colour.

8
00:00:21.443 --> 00:00:24.369
Frequency is how many per second, so pitch.

9
00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:26.886
Reflection bounces, refraction bends.

10
00:00:27.336 --> 00:00:31.515
Amplitude is the height of the wave: how far it moves from rest.

11
00:00:31.515 --> 00:00:35.433
In sound we experience it as loudness, measured in decibels.

12
00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:37.000
In light, as brightness.

13
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.773
Wavelength is the distance from one peak to the next.

14
00:00:40.773 --> 00:00:43.219
In light, it decides the colour we see.

15
00:00:43.219 --> 00:00:45.727
Visible light is measured in nanometres.

16
00:00:46.177 --> 00:00:50.789
Frequency is the number of waves passing per second, measured in hertz.

17
00:00:50.789 --> 00:00:53.582
As frequency goes up, wavelength goes down.

18
00:00:53.582 --> 00:00:55.986
In sound, we hear frequency as pitch.

19
00:00:55.986 --> 00:00:59.233
Humans hear about twenty to twenty thousand hertz.

20
00:01:00.683 --> 00:01:02.000
Light versus sound.

21
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.780
Light is electromagnetic, and needs no medium: it can cross a vacuum.

22
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.977
Sound is a mechanical pressure wave, made of compressions and rarefactions,

23
00:01:11.977 --> 00:01:15.510
and it needs a medium: a gas, a liquid, or a solid.

24
00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.590
Reflection: light bounces back off a flat, opaque surface, like a mirror.

25
00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:25.283
A coloured object reflects only its own wavelengths, and absorbs the rest.

26
00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:30.474
Refraction: light bends when it passes into a medium of a different density,

27
00:01:30.474 --> 00:01:32.221
because it slows down there.

28
00:01:32.221 --> 00:01:33.656
A rainbow in raindrops.

29
00:01:33.656 --> 00:01:38.959
And in your own eye, the cornea and the lens refract light to focus it on the retina.

30
00:01:39.409 --> 00:01:44.200
Her checklist says reflection and refraction of sound, too, and her keys cover it.

31
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.706
Sound reflects: an echo is reflected sound, and so is sonar.

32
00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:51.094
And sound refracts: it bends upward, away from hot ground,

33
00:01:51.094 --> 00:01:54.249
because it travels faster in hot air than in cold air.

34
00:01:54.249 --> 00:01:58.456
While we're on rainbows, her model answer: raindrops act as tiny prisms,

35
00:01:58.456 --> 00:02:03.130
and each wavelength of light slows by a different amount, so blue, the shortest,

36
00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:05.116
bends the most, and red the least.

37
00:02:06.566 --> 00:02:07.236
The traps.

38
00:02:07.236 --> 00:02:10.319
Loudness is amplitude, and pitch is frequency.

39
00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:12.665
Her quizzes swap them to catch you.

40
00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:16.283
Second: a higher frequency means a shorter wavelength.

41
00:02:16.283 --> 00:02:19.165
Third: sound can't travel through a vacuum.

42
00:02:19.165 --> 00:02:19.835
Light can.

43
00:02:20.285 --> 00:02:21.045
<v Examiner>Once more.

44
00:02:21.045 --> 00:02:21.501
<v Examiner>Waves.

45
00:02:23.951 --> 00:02:28.150
Amplitude is the wave's height: loudness in decibels, or brightness.

46
00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:31.115
Wavelength is peak to peak: the colour of light.

47
00:02:31.115 --> 00:02:34.820
Frequency is waves per second, in hertz: the pitch of sound,

48
00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:37.907
and a higher frequency means a shorter wavelength.

49
00:02:37.907 --> 00:02:40.686
Light is electromagnetic and needs no medium;

50
00:02:40.686 --> 00:02:43.836
sound is a mechanical pressure wave that needs one.

51
00:02:43.836 --> 00:02:46.676
Reflection: light bounces back, like a mirror.

52
00:02:46.676 --> 00:02:51.308
Refraction: light bends as it slows in a different density, like a rainbow,

53
00:02:51.308 --> 00:02:53.654
or the cornea and lens focusing light.

54
00:02:53.654 --> 00:02:58.533
Sound reflects as an echo or sonar, and refracts, bending away from hot ground.
